ainst", -0.0043299226090312], [" this", -6.099124908447266]]}, {"t": ":", "lp": -0.6113327145576477, "top": [[":", -0.6113327145576477], [" your", -0.8267468214035034]]}, {"t": "\n", "lp": -0.6749480366706848, "top": [["\n", -0.6749480366706848], ["\n\n", -0.7117418050765991]]}, {"t": "-", "lp": -0.021054483950138092, "top": [["-", -0.021054483950138092], ["1", -3.8812992572784424]]}, {"t": " Your", "lp": -0.1504552662372589, "top": [[" Your", -0.1504552662372589], [" The", -2.3032548427581787]]}, {"t": " specific", "lp": -0.6304535269737244, "top": [[" specific", -0.6304535269737244], [" installed", -0.8173165321350098]]}, {"t": " driver", "lp": -0.21956658363342285, "top": [[" driver", -0.21956658363342285], [" kernel", -2.146698236465454]]}, {"t": " version", "lp": -0.03328701853752136, "top": [[" version", -0.03328701853752136], ["'s", -3.5121116638183594]]}, {"t": "'s", "lp": -0.001525313127785921, "top": [["'s", -0.001525313127785921], [" (`", -7.21387243270874]]}, {"t": " documentation", "lp": -0.1062127873301506, "top": [[" documentation", -0.1062127873301506], [" `", -3.137676477432251]]}, {"t": "\n", "lp": -0.008785882033407688, "top": [["\n", -0.008785882033407688], [" (`", -4.87279748916626]]}], "text": "# CCS Modes on Intel Arc (xe driver)\n\nThe `ccs_mode` parameter controls how Compute Slice (CCS) contexts are handled by the Xe driver. Here's what each mode does:\n\n## CCS Mode 1\n- **Context stealing**: The driver will \"steal\" a CCS from another GPU context when your context needs one but none are available\n- More flexible but can cause contention issues\n- Generally the safer default for most workloads\n\n## CCS Mode 2\n- **No stealing**: The driver will not steal CCS from other contexts\n- If no CCS is available, your context may fail or fall back\n- More predictable behavior but can hit resource limits sooner\n\n## CCS Mode 4\n- **Explicit/forced CCS allocation**: Requests CCS explicitly and will fail rather than silently falling back\n- Useful when you want **deterministic behavior** and need to know exactly when CCS is or isn't available\n\n## When to use each\n\n| Mode | Use case |\n|------|----------|\n| **1** | General default; best compatibility |\n| **2** | When you want to avoid contention from stealing |\n| **4** | Debugging, testing, or when you need explicit control over CCS allocation |\n\n## Practical notes\n\n- Most users should stick with **mode 1** unless they're debugging specific issues\n- Mode 4 is particularly useful when **diagnosing** CCS-related failures or when you want to verify your workload's CCS requirements\n- The exact semantics can vary slightly between driver versions, so check your specific `xe` driver documentation\n\n---\n\n**A caveat**: I want to be transparent that my knowledge of the exact `ccs_mode` semantics in the Xe driver may not be perfectly precise, and these parameters have evolved. I'd recommend verifying against:\n- Your specific driver version's documentation\n"}